-- FINDING 2 (silent).  Pin only the system time (the reading day) and `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE` becomes the
-- reading day.  It is the documented fallback chain (explicit rule-effective pin, else valid-time pin, else
-- today, where "today" is read from system time), and it is the wrong default for this case: a caller who
-- carries the conduct day as an ordinary parameter and pins only the reading day gets the law in force on the
-- READING day applied to the conduct, with no diagnostic.
--
-- Printed: line 1 gives (1 Aug 2012, 1 Aug 2012).  The conduct day, 1 Jun 2011, never reached the rule.
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
TIMEZONE IS "Asia/Singapore"

`the day whose text governs` MEANS `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`
`the day the law is read` MEANS TODAY

-- system time only: both come out as the reading day
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (LIST `the day whose text governs`, `the day the law is read`)
-- valid time as well: the text day follows the conduct day, as intended
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 8 2012) (`EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2011) (LIST `the day whose text governs`, `the day the law is read`))
